			"44": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "FRICTION\n\nSo we got our movement fundamental and we are adding the flocking motion with our movement noise.\nNext we apply some friction to the dots by using the Smooth node.\nThe smooth node will Smooth the transition between values over a given time(duration).\nBut instead of using a single duration, which would simply delay all instances the same, we use 100 different instances.\nThis means that each dot gets smoothed by a different amount.\nWe shuffle the linear node to make sure that which circle gets smoothed is random.\n\n\nSet the control mode to either Sine or Perlin and play with the amount of friction. \nYou'll see that some dots are dragging behind the main flock.\nThis gives the feeling that not all dots are equally fast.\n\nAfter you're done playing, move up to the  Shape Generation bit."
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 589.8470458984375,
					"width": 972.16851806640625,
					"x": 2.98575973510742188,
					"y": 15.503509521484375
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "ffe53357",
				"constants": {},
				"hidden": [
					"text",
					"alignment",
					"font-size",
					"fill"
				],
				"name": "Comment"
			},
			"45": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "MOVEMENT NOISE\n\nAfter we've created a X\/Y fundamental for our flock it is time to actually start flocking!\nWe do this by having two Perlin Noise oscillators for each instance of the circle node (the dots are made by circle nodes).\nOne oscillator for X and one for Y , but they all share the Noise Min, Noise Max and Noise Frequency parameters.\n\nWe use a Linear node to set the amplitude of the oscillators. This way some dots will move further out of the fundamental than others, resulting in a more organic look. Try setting Noise Min and Max to the same value and after that set Min to 0 and Max  to 1 to see the difference.\n\nThe Noise Frequency is there just to change how fast the values are changing.\nA Linear is used, again, to make things less uniform."
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 713.523193359375,
					"width": 1146.61572265625,
					"x": -1928.0809326171875,
					"y": 671.6868896484375
				},
